# Line endings: the repository stores text files with LF, and Git writes them with the operating system's line
# ending (CRLF on Windows, LF on Linux and macOS) whenever it clones, pulls or checks out. Files pinned to LF
# below stay LF everywhere.
#
# Files already on disk are not rewritten when these rules change. To make the repository and every file match
# the rules, exactly as a fresh clone, commit .gitattributes and your changes, then run once in the repository root:
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git commit -m "Normalize line endings"
#   git rm --cached -r -q .
#   git reset --hard

* text=auto

# Shell scripts and Dockerfiles MUST stay LF even on Windows checkout. When
# a Windows dev clone has `core.autocrlf=true`, the `text=auto` rule above
# converts them to CRLF in the working tree; Docker COPY then carries CRLF
# into the image and Linux's kernel can't exec the shebang (#!/bin/bash\r),
# so the container exits 1 with no log output. Pin LF explicitly.
*.sh         text eol=lf
*.bash       text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
# The shared cross-platform job runner is run via python on both Linux and
# Windows; keep it LF so the container copy never carries CRLF.
Files/Docker/ai-dev-agent/run-agent-task.py text eol=lf
Files/Docker/ai-dev-agent/agent-poll.py text eol=lf
Files/Docker/ai-dev-agent/clean_jobs.py text eol=lf

# Mark common binary formats so Git never tries EOL normalization/diff heuristics
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.7z   binary
*.gz   binary
*.tar  binary
*.dll  binary
*.exe  binary
